# shellcheck shell=bash
#
# shared/brew.lib -- Intent's Homebrew formula: what it names, how it is
# rendered, and the one push to the tap (ST0081 WP-06). SOURCED, never run: 0644
# so the dispatcher's `is_handler` (-f AND -x) cannot mistake it for a command.
#
# WHY THIS IS SHARED. The formula lived in `cmd/macos` while the release was
# macOS arm64 alone, and its header said it would move once Linux artefacts
# arrived. They have: the formula names the Linux packages for x86_64 and aarch64
# beside the macOS assets, so `int brew render` and `int brew publish` both
# render it here, and the formula a release pushes is the one the verb prints.
#
# FROM THE SOURCING HANDLER IT NEEDS: helpers (die, require_tool), rust_host from
# shared/rusthost.lib (staged_version names the staged binary with it), and ROOT.

# THE SUPPORT TREE, AND WHY A RELEASE OF BINARIES ALSO SHIPS A TARBALL.
#
# **A Rust binary cannot carry shell inside itself.** Intent ships its Claude Code
# hooks and its whiteboard guards as FILES under `lib/templates/`, and shipped
# consumers exec them out of the install tree: `intent claude hook <name>` runs
# `lib/templates/.claude/scripts/<name>.sh`, and the pre-commit gate runs
# `lib/templates/hooks/<guard>.sh` at a path it parses back out of `intent info`.
# The binary finds that tree by walking up from its own symlink-resolved location
# to the directory containing `lib/templates/` (`intentsvcs::install::MARKER`),
# with no environment fallback -- deliberately, because a stale v2 `$INTENT_HOME`
# would otherwise make a v3 binary exec v2's hook scripts.
#
# A formula that installs only the binaries therefore produces an install that
# cannot find itself. Measured against a reproduction of exactly what the formula
# builds -- binary under `Cellar/intent/3.0.0/bin/`, brew-style symlink, neutral
# cwd (probe at 501f5083): `intent info` and `intent claude hook` both fail, and
# the gate's guard path comes back unresolvable. **Every consumer project would
# silently lose its session hooks and both whiteboard guards.**
#
# THE TARBALL'S ROOT IS THE INSTALL ROOT, one level under a wrapper directory,
# and that is the one design decision here worth defending. **The wrapper is not
# decoration -- see SUPPORT_PREFIX in cmd/macos. Without it this archive installs to the wrong
# depth and the install cannot find itself, which is the exact failure the
# paragraph above says the archive exists to prevent. Measured on the first real
# brew install, 2026-08-26.** It contains `lib/templates/...`
# rather than `templates/...`, so the formula's install line is "put everything in
# this archive into libexec" rather than "put templates in libexec/lib". The rule
# library and the skills tree that `intent critic` and `intent claude rules` read
# live OUTSIDE the marker directory (`intent/plugins/claude/`), and rooting the
# archive at the install root is what lets a tree like that join the shipped set
# as a CONTENT change, not a formula change and not a second asset.
#
# IT IS NOT SIGNED AND NOT NOTARISED, AND THAT IS NOT AN OMISSION. Notarisation
# assesses Mach-O binaries; a gzipped tar of shell and markdown has nothing to
# sign and nowhere to hold a ticket. The control that protects it is the same one
# that protects the binaries: `publish` re-downloads what it uploaded and hashes
# THAT. What this DOES require is that `checksum` stop treating "staged artefact"
# and "must be proven notarised" as the same set -- see cmd_checksum in cmd/macos, where an
# unclassified artefact is now a refusal rather than a default.
SUPPORT_ASSET="intent-support.tar.gz"

# THE APP SHIPS AS A RELEASE ASSET BESIDE THE CLI (decision 7; hv: "Of course
# the app ships"). A zip of the STAPLED bundle, made by `macos stage` with `ditto
# -c -k --keepParent` so the signature, the ticket and the extended attributes
# survive. NO TRIPLE: the bundle is universal (x86_64 arm64), so a host triple
# would be false. NO VERSION, as for SUPPORT_ASSET: the tag carries it. Unlike
# the support tarball it IS notarised, so `macos checksum` proves it before
# hashing it. The formula stays CLI-only; the cask names this asset (issue 0634).
APP_ASSET="Intent.app.zip"

# WHERE THE ARTEFACTS GO AND WHERE THE CLAIM ABOUT THEM GOES. Separate repositories,
# by hv's ruling of 2026-08-15 (D40): artefacts live on the source repo's own
# releases, and the tap carries only the formula that names them.
#
# The spellings are all real and none is derivable from the others without
# a rule someone has to remember: GitHub calls the tap `homebrew-intent`, brew
# calls the same thing `matthewsinclair/intent`, and the source repo is a third
# name again. Written out once rather than reconstructed at each use.
REPO="matthewsinclair/intent"
TAP="matthewsinclair/homebrew-intent"
# shellcheck disable=SC2034 # read by the handlers that source this file
BREW_TAP="matthewsinclair/intent"

# MEASURED 2026-08-15: neither `intent` nor `intentd` exists in homebrew-core, so
# the names are free and `brew install intent` would resolve to our tap once it
# is tapped. **We still print the fully-qualified `matthewsinclair/intent/intent`
# everywhere, deliberately.** The short form works only while core stays empty of
# that name, and the day core gains one, every published instruction becomes
# ambiguous at best and installs somebody else's software at worst -- in an
# instruction users have already copied into their own docs. The long form cannot
# be shadowed and costs one line of typing.

# EVERYTHING AFTER THE COPY HAPPENS HERE, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE POINT.
#
# `target/release/` is SHARED MUTABLE STATE. Several sessions build in this tree and
# `cargo build --release` is the most ordinary thing any of them does -- so a
# signature living there is not a property of a file, it is a property of a file
# that nobody has rebuilt yet.
#
# MEASURED LIVE, 2026-08-15: a peer's release build replaced a Developer ID
# signature with the linker's ad-hoc one, and one of the shipped binaries stopped
# being notarised inside the hour, with NO signal and every artefact of the
# earlier proof still reading as valid. Nobody did anything wrong; a shared target
# directory is what it is for.
#
# The first response was to make `stage` REFUSE to checksum anything it could not
# prove. That was right and it stays -- but a refusal DETECTS the race, it does
# not remove it, and it detects it only at the end, after a notarisation round
# trip has already been spent on bytes that no longer exist.
#
# So the target moved instead. `target/dist/` is written by `int macos stage` and by
# nothing else, which means the window in which a peer can disturb an artefact
# shrinks from "the whole sign -> notarise -> stage sequence", minutes long and
# spanning a network round trip to Apple, to "the ditto on the line below". **A
# structure that cannot fail beats a control that catches the failure**, and the
# control is still there underneath because catching it is free.
STAGE_DIR="$ROOT/native/rust/target/dist"

# require_sums -- the proof file, or the reason there isn't one.
#
# ONE MESSAGE FOR ONE MISSING FILE. `formula` and `publish` both need it and both
# need to say the same thing about why it is absent, because the answer is never
# "create it" -- it is "prove the artefacts and it will be written for you".
require_sums() {
  [ -f "$STAGE_DIR/SHA256SUMS.txt" ] ||
    die "no proven artefacts to describe -- run 'bin/devbin macos prepare' first (SHA256SUMS.txt is written only by 'checksum', and only for artefacts it has proven signed AND notarised)"
}

# staged_version -- the version the SHIPPING binary reports.
#
# From the BINARY, never from Cargo.toml or a git tag. It is the only source that
# cannot disagree with what a user will actually run: a version read from a
# manifest describes what was meant to be built, and the gap between those two is
# exactly what a release must not ship. It also makes the formula's `test do`
# block pass by construction rather than by coincidence.
staged_version() {
  local bin out v
  # THE VERSION TOKEN ONLY. `--version` prints `intent <version> (<commit>)`, and
  # the provenance suffix is NOT part of the version. Taking everything after
  # `intent ` put `3.0.0-dev (4c5babdf...)` into `version`, into RELEASE_VERSION,
  # and therefore into every download URL -- a URL carrying a space and
  # parentheses, inside a formula that was otherwise perfectly well-formed and
  # linted clean. Nothing about the output looked wrong.
  #
  # FOUND ON THE FIRST END-TO-END RUN, which is precisely what the cmd_formula
  # header then predicted would happen to this path: "this path has no end-to-end
  # test, and refactoring an unexercised artefact for elegance is how the first
  # real run finds something new". It found something new.
  #
  # AND EVERY WAY THE READ FAILS SPEAKS (issue 0595's census). This was one pipe,
  # `"$bin" --version 2>/dev/null | sed`, under `set -euo pipefail` and helpers'
  # inherit_errexit, so a staged binary that was missing or would not run ended
  # the script at the pipe, in silence, before the die below could say why: an
  # empty refusal at a release cut. Its stderr is kept, because a binary that will
  # not run says why there.
  bin="$STAGE_DIR/intent-$(rust_host)"
  [ -e "$bin" ] || die "there is no staged intent binary at $bin -- run 'bin/devbin macos stage' (or 'prepare') first"
  out="$("$bin" --version 2>&1)" || die "the staged intent binary does not run, so its version cannot be read. $bin --version said:
$out"
  v="$(printf '%s\n' "$out" | sed -n 's/^intent \([^ ]*\).*/\1/p')"
  [ -n "$v" ] || die "could not read a version from the staged intent binary. $bin --version said:
$out"
  # A VERSION THAT CANNOT SIT IN A URL IS A BROKEN FORMULA, and it does not break
  # for us -- it breaks for every `brew install`, against a formula already
  # published. Refuse where the harm is, the same rule that keeps the dev-version
  # refusal in `publish`.
  case "$v" in
    *[!A-Za-z0-9.+_-]*)
      die "the staged binary reports a version that is not URL-safe: '$v' -- the formula's download URLs are built from it"
      ;;
  esac
  printf '%s' "$v"
}

# --- the asset set -----------------------------------------------------------
# The macOS assets' triple. The formula's macOS branch is arm64 only, by hv's
# ruling of 2026-08-15, so this is a constant and not this host's triple: the
# formula is rendered on Linux too (a dev tap on hv's Ubuntu VM, or a CI step),
# where the host's own triple names nothing on the release.
MACOS_TRIPLE="aarch64-apple-darwin"
# The Linux packages' triples, one package per architecture, named as `int linux
# package` names them (ST0081 WP-01).
LINUX_TRIPLES="x86_64-unknown-linux-musl aarch64-unknown-linux-musl"

# brew_macos_assets -- the three macOS assets the formula names, one per line:
# the CLI, the daemon and the support tree.
brew_macos_assets() {
  printf '%s\n' "intent-$MACOS_TRIPLE" "intentd-$MACOS_TRIPLE" "$SUPPORT_ASSET"
}

# brew_linux_asset <triple> -- the Linux package for one architecture.
brew_linux_asset() {
  printf 'intent-%s.tar.gz\n' "$1"
}

# brew_sum <sums> <asset> -- PURE. The sha256 a `<sha256>  <asset>` file gives
# <asset>, or nothing.
brew_sum() {
  awk -v f="$2" '$2 == f { print $1; exit }' "$1"
}

# brew_url <base> <asset> -- PURE. Where the formula sends brew for <asset>: the
# release's own download URL through RELEASE_VERSION when <base> is `release`,
# and <base>/<asset> otherwise.
brew_url() {
  if [ "$1" = release ]; then
    printf 'https://github.com/%s/releases/download/v#{RELEASE_VERSION}/%s\n' "$REPO" "$2"
  else
    printf '%s/%s\n' "$1" "$2"
  fi
}

# sha256_of <file> -- IMPURE. The file's sha256: sha256sum where it exists (GNU,
# so Linux), else shasum (perl's, so macOS). Refuses when neither is on PATH.
sha256_of() {
  local out
  if command -v sha256sum >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    out="$(sha256sum "$1")" || die "sha256sum could not read $1"
  elif command -v shasum >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    out="$(shasum -a 256 "$1")" || die "shasum could not read $1"
  else
    die "neither sha256sum nor shasum is on PATH, so no asset can be hashed"
  fi
  printf '%s\n' "${out%% *}"
}

# brew_fetch <from> <dir> <asset>... -- IMPURE. Fetch each asset from
# <from>/<asset> into <dir>, and print `<sha256>  <asset>` for each one that came
# back. Each one that did not is printed to stderr as `missing: <asset> from
# <url> (<curl's words>)`, and the return is 3 when any is missing. It never stops
# at the first: a refusal names every asset it lacks (vc, 2026-10-14).
brew_fetch() {
  local from="$1" dir="$2" a url err h rc=0
  shift 2
  for a in "$@"; do
    url="$from/$a"
    if err="$(curl -fsSL -o "$dir/$a" "$url" 2>&1)"; then
      # return, never exit: brew_formula runs this with stderr in a file it shows
      # only on its own way out, so an exit here would drop sha256_of's refusal
      # unread and leave the fetch directory behind (vc, 2026-10-14).
      h="$(sha256_of "$dir/$a")" || return 1
      printf '%s  %s\n' "$h" "$a"
    else
      printf 'missing: %s from %s (%s)\n' "$a" "$url" "${err:-curl exited non-zero}" >&2
      rc=3
    fi
  done
  return "$rc"
}

# THE FORMULA -- rendered, never hand-edited, from the bytes at a base URL.
#
# GENERATED, NEVER HAND-EDITED, and that is the whole point. A tap formula
# carries a version and one sha256 per artefact, and every one of them is a
# restatement of a fact that already exists somewhere else. Conflab hand-maintains
# exactly these lines and has a `release sync` command partly to heal the drift
# that follows. **A number copied by hand is a number that will eventually be
# copied wrong**, and this particular wrong number does not fail for us -- it
# fails for every `brew install`, against a formula we already published.
#
# EVERY HASH IS READ FROM THE BYTES THE BASE URL SERVES (ST0081 WP-06). The only
# input used to be `SHA256SUMS.txt`, which `checksum` writes ONLY for artefacts it
# has proven signed and notarised, so there was no path from an unproven binary to
# a formula. The Linux packages are not in that file, being built on Linux, so
# every hash now comes from what the release (or a dev tap's file:// base) serves,
# the round trip `publish` already made for macOS. The macOS half of the property
# holds: with the release's own base, each macOS hash must equal the one
# SHA256SUMS.txt records, and `publish` uploads only what `checksum` proved.
#
# THE DEV-VERSION REFUSAL IS DELIBERATELY NOT HERE. Emitting a formula that reads
# `RELEASE_VERSION = "3.0.0-dev"` is harmless -- it is text on a terminal, and it is
# self-evidently not a release. The harm is in PUBLISHING one, so the refusal
# belongs in the publish step. **Put the control where the harm is, not where it
# is convenient to write.**
#
# VALIDATED WITH `brew style`, AND THE CALIBRATION IS THE POINT. Linted as a bare
# file the generated formula reports several offences; linted AT A TAP PATH it
# reports far fewer. The difference was artefacts of judging a formula by the
# generic Ruby config -- Sorbet sigils, a frozen-string-literal comment,
# top-level class documentation -- none of which Homebrew applies inside a tap.
# **Calibrated against `geodica/homebrew-conflab`'s live shipped formula**, which
# reports the same remaining offence and none of the extras, then confirmed by linting a
# copy of ours at a real tap path. `Style/MutableConstant` is now fixed here, so
# this generates a formula that lints clean where the reference does not.
#
# **A linter run in the wrong context reports real-looking findings about a
# correct artefact**, and the only way to tell was to run it against something
# already known good. Same shape as `spctl -a -t exec` calling a correctly signed
# binary rejected.
#
# THE CONSTANT ITSELF STAYS, deliberately. `RELEASE_VERSION` is the formula's
# only statement of the version -- there is no `version` line, because brew reads
# it from the /v<version>/ path -- and every download URL interpolates it.
# `#{version}` may well work in its place, but the constant is what the shipped
# conflab formula does, and refactoring an artefact for elegance is how the next
# real run finds something new.
# A formula rendered from any other base, such as a dev tap's file:// URLs, has
# no /v<version>/ for brew to read, so it states the version on a line of its own.
#
# MOVED, AS ITS EXPIRY NOTE SAID IT WOULD BE (ST0081 WP-06). This lived under
# `macos` while hv's ruling of 2026-08-15 made the release macOS arm64 only, and
# its note said Linux artefacts would end that limit and the reason for it. hv
# widened the release to Linux on 2026-10-14, so the formula grew per-platform
# URLs and moved here, where `int brew render` and `int brew publish` both
# render it.

# brew_render_formula <version> <revision> <base> <sums> <commit> -- PURE. The
# formula on stdout, for the assets <sums> names (`<sha256>  <asset>` lines).
# <base> is `release` for the release's own download URLs, written through
# RELEASE_VERSION, or a literal base URL, which also gets a version line.
# <commit> is the commit the assets were built from, read from git by the
# caller and written into the manifest the keg carries (ST0087 T3).
#
# THE BRANCHES COME FROM WHAT <sums> CARRIES, so how complete a formula must be is
# the caller's rule: the macOS branch needs all three macOS assets, and each Linux
# architecture its own package. A formula without the macOS branch depends on
# Linux, one without a Linux branch depends on macOS, and one with a single Linux
# architecture depends on it, so a partial dev tap refuses on a platform it does
# not describe rather than install the wrong bytes there.
brew_render_formula() {
  local version="$1" revision="$2" base="$3" sums="$4" commit="$5"
  local t pkg mac=1 linux="" sha_cli sha_daemon sha_support top_url top_sha
  sha_cli="$(brew_sum "$sums" "intent-$MACOS_TRIPLE")"
  sha_daemon="$(brew_sum "$sums" "intentd-$MACOS_TRIPLE")"
  sha_support="$(brew_sum "$sums" "$SUPPORT_ASSET")"
  if [ -z "$sha_cli" ] || [ -z "$sha_daemon" ] || [ -z "$sha_support" ]; then
    mac=0
  fi
  for t in $LINUX_TRIPLES; do
    if [ -n "$(brew_sum "$sums" "$(brew_linux_asset "$t")")" ]; then
      linux="$linux $t"
    fi
  done
  linux="${linux# }"
  [ "$mac" -eq 1 ] || [ -n "$linux" ] ||
    die "no platform's assets are all there, so there is no formula to render"
  if [ "$mac" -eq 1 ]; then
    top_url="$(brew_url "$base" "intent-$MACOS_TRIPLE")"
    top_sha="$sha_cli"
  else
    pkg="$(brew_linux_asset "${linux%% *}")"
    top_url="$(brew_url "$base" "$pkg")"
    top_sha="$(brew_sum "$sums" "$pkg")"
  fi

  printf 'class Intent < Formula\n'
  if [ "$base" != release ]; then
    # The base is not repeated here: its url lines already name it, and `brew
    # style` lets a long url line pass and refuses a long comment.
    printf '  # NOT THE RELEASE FORMULA. Rendered by `bin/int brew render` for a tap that\n'
    printf '  # proves the formula before anything is pushed: the URLs below are not the\n'
    printf "  # release's.\n"
  fi
  cat <<FORMULA
  RELEASE_VERSION = "$version".freeze
  SOURCE_COMMIT = "$commit".freeze

  desc "Steel thread process for helping LLMs help you work with your code"
  homepage "https://github.com/$REPO"
  # THE URL IS AT TOP LEVEL, NOT INSIDE \`on_macos do on_arm do\`. Until 3.0.1 it
  # was nested there, and \`brew tap\` validates a tap's formulae under EVERY
  # simulated OS and arch: on Linux and on Intel the nested form has no url, so
  # the tap refused with "formula requires at least a URL" and "invalid syntax
  # in tap", for everyone.
FORMULA
  if [ "$mac" -eq 1 ]; then
    cat <<'FORMULA'
  # It is macOS arm64's, and on Linux the package for the machine's
  # architecture overrides it inside `on_linux`, below.
FORMULA
  else
    cat <<'FORMULA'
  # This formula renders no macOS branch, so it is the Linux package's.
FORMULA
  fi
  if [ "$base" = release ]; then
    cat <<'FORMULA'
  # There is no `version` line because brew reads it from the /v<version>/ path,
  # and `brew audit --strict` refuses the redundant one.
FORMULA
  else
    cat <<'FORMULA'
  # These URLs are not the release's, so they carry no /v<version>/ for brew to
  # read, and the version line states it.
FORMULA
  fi
  printf '  url "%s"\n' "$top_url"
  [ "$base" = release ] || printf '  version "%s"\n' "$version"
  printf '  sha256 "%s"\n' "$top_sha"
  printf '  license "MIT"\n'
  [ "$revision" -eq 0 ] || printf '  revision %s\n' "$revision"
  cat <<'FORMULA'

  # On macOS it is arm64 only, by ruling (hv, 2026-08-15) rather than by
  # omission, and TRUE BY CONSTRUCTION through the dependency line inside
  # `on_macos`, which is what a platform limit in a formula is for. Both macOS
  # binaries are Developer ID signed and notarised; nothing is stapled, because a
  # bare Mach-O has nowhere to hold a ticket and Gatekeeper checks online. On
  # Linux (hv, 2026-10-14) the package for the machine's architecture carries
  # both binaries, built static against musl, and the support tree. (No comment
  # line here may open with the dependency keyword: `brew audit` reads that as a
  # commented-out dependency and refuses it.)
FORMULA
  if [ "$mac" -eq 0 ]; then
    printf '  depends_on :linux\n\n'
  elif [ -z "$linux" ]; then
    printf '  depends_on :macos\n\n'
  fi
  if [ "$mac" -eq 1 ]; then
    cat <<FORMULA
  on_macos do
    depends_on arch: :arm64

    resource "intentd" do
      url "$(brew_url "$base" "intentd-$MACOS_TRIPLE")"
      sha256 "$sha_daemon"
    end

    # The support tree the binaries read and exec: templates, hooks, guards, the
    # rule library, skills and subagents. Not signed and not notarised -- there
    # is no Mach-O in it. See SUPPORT_PATHS in bin/.devbin/cmd/shared/support.lib.
    resource "support" do
      url "$(brew_url "$base" "$SUPPORT_ASSET")"
      sha256 "$sha_support"
    end
  end

FORMULA
  fi
  if [ -n "$linux" ]; then
    printf '  on_linux do\n'
    case "$linux" in
      x86_64-*' '* | aarch64-*' '*) ;;
      x86_64-*) printf '    depends_on arch: :x86_64\n\n' ;;
      aarch64-*) printf '    depends_on arch: :arm64\n\n' ;;
    esac
    for t in $linux; do
      pkg="$(brew_linux_asset "$t")"
      case "$t" in
        x86_64-*) printf '    on_intel do\n' ;;
        aarch64-*) printf '    on_arm do\n' ;;
      esac
      printf '      url "%s"\n' "$(brew_url "$base" "$pkg")"
      printf '      sha256 "%s"\n' "$(brew_sum "$sums" "$pkg")"
      printf '    end\n'
    done
    printf '  end\n\n'
  fi
  cat <<'FORMULA'
  # EVERYTHING LANDS IN libexec AND bin GETS SYMLINKS, which is not the obvious
  # layout and is not a style preference.
  #
  # `intent` locates its own install by walking up from its symlink-resolved
  # location to the directory containing `lib/templates/`, so the support tree
  # has to sit beside the binary inside the keg -- a plain `bin.install` leaves
  # the binary unable to find the hooks it execs, and both whiteboard guards and
  # every session hook stop working with nothing reporting a failure.
  #
  # That rules IN the layouts that keep the tree in the keg, and Homebrew rules one of them OUT. Installing to
  # `prefix/lib/templates` resolves correctly, but `lib` is a LINKED directory:
  # brew symlinks keg `lib` subdirectories into the shared prefix, so this would
  # publish a directory called `templates` into `#{HOMEBREW_PREFIX}/lib`
  # alongside every other formula's entries. `templates` is about as generic as a
  # name gets, and the collision would surface as a `brew link` failure in
  # somebody else's install. `libexec` is not linked, so the tree stays private
  # to this keg.
  def install
    if OS.mac?
      (libexec/"bin").install Dir["intent-*"].first => "intent"
      resource("intentd").stage do
        (libexec/"bin").install Dir["intentd-*"].first => "intentd"
      end
      # The archive is rooted at the install root, so this stays correct when the
      # shipped set grows -- it installs whatever the tarball carries rather than
      # naming `templates` and having to change when something joins it.
      resource("support").stage do
        libexec.install Dir["*"]
      end
    else
      # The Linux package is the install root already, under the one top-level
      # directory brew strips when it unpacks: bin/intent and bin/intentd, and
      # the support tree beside them (bin/int linux package).
      libexec.install Dir["*"]
    end

    # THE EXECUTABLE BIT, AND IT IS NOT BELT-AND-BRACES -- WITHOUT IT THE KEG
    # INSTALLS AND NOTHING IN IT RUNS. GitHub serves release assets as plain
    # files with no exec bit, and Homebrew's `.install` PRESERVES the source
    # mode rather than setting one. So 644 goes in, 644 comes out, `brew
    # install` prints its beer emoji, and every `intent` call returns
    # `permission denied`. Measured on the v3.0.0 tap, 2026-08-26, on the first
    # real install from the network. Harmless on Linux, where tar keeps the mode.
    #
    # A CACHE-PRESEEDED INSTALL CANNOT CATCH THIS, which is why it survived
    # every proof taken before that one: a preseed fills the cache from LOCAL
    # files that are already +x, so the mode survives and the install works.
    # Only the network hop loses it. "Proves everything but the network hop"
    # was an honest scoping, and this is what was behind it.
    chmod 0755, libexec/"bin/intent"
    chmod 0755, libexec/"bin/intentd"

    bin.install_symlink libexec/"bin/intent"
    bin.install_symlink libexec/"bin/intentd"

    # THE MANIFEST (ST0087 T3), WRITTEN HERE BECAUSE THE KEG HAS NO CHECKOUT.
    # `intent install --managed-by brew` writes the same file for a tree it
    # publishes, and it runs only in a dev checkout, so the formula writes it
    # itself over what it laid out: the header, then one `shasum -a 256 -c` line
    # per file, every regular file under libexec, sorted bytewise. T3 fixes the
    # form and not the writer. `managed_by: brew` is what makes `intent doctor`
    # and `intent use` read this keg as the brew tree and refuse to write into it.
    # It is the end of `install` and not a post_install, because Homebrew 7's
    # `brew style` refuses post_install for the declarative post_install_steps,
    # which have no step that can hash a file. The binaries are prebuilt, signed
    # and static, so nothing brew does after `install` rewrites them, and doctor
    # names any listed file that changed or went missing since.
    manifest = libexec/".intent-install"
    files = Dir.glob("**/*", File::FNM_DOTMATCH, base: libexec).select do |f|
      (libexec/f).file? && libexec/f != manifest
    end
    lines = [
      "# tool: intent",
      "# version: #{RELEASE_VERSION}",
      "# source_commit: #{SOURCE_COMMIT}",
      "# managed_by: brew",
    ]
    lines += files.sort.map { |f| "#{(libexec/f).sha256}  #{f}" }
    manifest.write("#{lines.join("\n")}\n")
  end

  # A CAVEAT, NOT A post_install, AND THE CHOICE WAS DRIVEN (dc, 2026-09-11).
  # The pre-commit gate a project installs finds this keg through ~/.local/share/intent/home,
  # and only `intent bootstrap` writes that file, so a fresh install refuses
  # every commit until it runs. A post_install cannot run it for the user:
  # Homebrew gives post_install a throwaway HOME inside its sandbox and denies
  # writes outside the keg, measured with a probe formula (Dir.home was a
  # sandbox temp directory; a write to the real home failed with EPERM). The
  # caveat is conditional because bootstrap REPOINTS an existing pointer, and a
  # machine already set up -- a source checkout, say -- should not be moved.
  #
  # THE UPGRADE CASE (issue 0527, 2026-09-23). Intent 3.2.0 and earlier recorded
  # the versioned keg, Cellar/intent/<version>/libexec, and `brew upgrade`
  # deletes that keg, so the pointer names nothing and every gated commit is
  # refused. From 3.2.1, bootstrap records opt/intent/libexec, which follows
  # every upgrade. No 3.2.1 code runs before the old pointer breaks, so this
  # caveat is the one lever that reaches such a machine at its upgrade. It stays
  # conditional: a pointer naming anything else is left as it is.
  #
  # **WHETHER THE KEG IS STILL ON DISK DOES NOT DECIDE IT** (issue 0540). This
  # said to run bootstrap only once the keg the pointer named was gone, and a
  # keg brew has not cleaned up yet still resolves, so that machine read the
  # advice as not its own and broke at the next `brew cleanup`. A pointer
  # naming any keg under Cellar/intent/ is re-recorded, and `intent bootstrap
  # --check` (issue 0533) is how a reader sees which case they are in.
  def caveats
    <<~EOS
      Intent's pre-commit gate finds this install through ~/.local/share/intent/home, which
      only `intent bootstrap` writes, and `intent bootstrap --check` says where it points.
      Run
        intent bootstrap
      if that file does not exist yet, or if it names an Intent under Cellar/intent/, whether
      or not that version is still installed: Intent 3.2.0 and earlier recorded the versioned
      keg, which `brew upgrade` removes, and from then on every commit in a project with the
      gate installed is refused. Later versions record a path that survives upgrades, and a
      file naming anything else, such as a source checkout, needs nothing.

      A maintainer with a dev or opt tree beside this keg chooses which one answers
      `intent` with `intent use`, and `intent use brew` hands it back to this keg.
    EOS
  end

  # THE SERVICE BLOCK, ADDED 2026-09-05 (dc) BECAUSE ITS OWN STATED PRECONDITION
  # WAS MET. This comment read: no start verb and no log path, so any block here
  # would be a guess at an interface that does not exist. Both halves are now
  # false and were driven on the delivered pair rather than inferred --
  # `intent daemon start|stop|status|run` all answer, start is idempotent and
  # reports its pid, and the log path exists and is being written.
  #
  # IT RUNS `intentd` DIRECTLY RATHER THAN `intent daemon run`, on the daemon's
  # own words: main.rs says serving is what this binary does with NO arguments,
  # and that `intent daemon run` EXECS this binary. Going through the CLI would
  # add a process for launchd to supervise in front of the one that matters, and
  # supervise the wrong one if the exec ever became a spawn.
  #
  # THE LOG PATHS ARE BREW'S, NOT THE DAEMON'S, AND THAT IS NOT A DUPLICATE.
  # intentd writes its own structured log under the user's state dir; these
  # capture stdout and stderr, both into one brew log, where main.rs puts the
  # warnings a supervised process would otherwise drop on the floor. The brew log
  # and the daemon's log are different files with different subjects. On Linux
  # `brew services` runs it under systemd's user manager, which no run has
  # proven yet.
  service do
    run [opt_bin/"intentd"]
    keep_alive true
    log_path var/"log/intentd.log"
    error_log_path var/"log/intentd.log"
  end

  test do
    assert_match version.to_s, shell_output("#{bin}/intent --version")
  end
end
FORMULA
}

# brew_source_commit <version> <dev> -- the commit the keg's manifest names: the
# tag v<version>'s, read from git, never typed (T8). A dev tap renders before
# its tag exists, from packages built at HEAD, so with <dev> 1 and no tag it is
# HEAD's, and stderr says so. A release formula refuses without its tag.
brew_source_commit() {
  local version="$1" dev="$2" sha
  if sha="$(git -C "$ROOT" rev-parse --verify --quiet "v$version^{commit}")"; then
    printf '%s\n' "$sha"
  elif [ "$dev" -eq 1 ] && sha="$(git -C "$ROOT" rev-parse --verify --quiet HEAD)"; then
    printf '%s: no tag v%s here, so the dev tap'"'"'s manifest names HEAD, %s\n' "$DEVBIN_NAME" "$version" "$sha" >&2
    printf '%s\n' "$sha"
  else
    die "no tag v$version in $ROOT, so there is no commit for the keg's manifest to name: render a release formula in a checkout that carries the tag"
  fi
}

# brew_formula <version> <revision> <from> <dev> -- IMPURE. Formula/intent.rb on
# stdout, every sha256 read from the bytes at <from>, which is the release's
# download URL for <version> when <from> is empty. A release formula needs all
# five assets: when any is not there the return is 3, every missing asset having
# been named with its URL on stderr, and nothing is rendered. With <dev> 1 it
# renders the platforms whose assets are all there and names the others.
#
# WITH THE RELEASE'S OWN BASE, each macOS hash must equal the one SHA256SUMS.txt
# records, when that file is staged: `checksum` writes it only for artefacts it
# has proven signed and notarised, so a formula cannot describe macOS bytes that
# proof did not cover.
#
# WRITTEN WITHOUT LEANING ON errexit, because its callers capture it, and
# `x="$(f)" || rc=$?` runs f as a condition: bash suspends `set -e` inside that
# substitution too, so a step that relied on it would carry on after failing.
# Every step here checks its own status.
brew_formula() {
  local version="$1" revision="$2" from="$3" dev="$4" base tmp rc=0 a t got want staged assets commit
  require_tool curl
  if [ -z "$from" ]; then
    base=release
    from="https://github.com/$REPO/releases/download/v$version"
  else
    base="$from"
  fi
  tmp="$(mktemp -d)" || die "could not make a directory to fetch the assets into"
  assets="$(brew_macos_assets)"
  for t in $LINUX_TRIPLES; do
    assets="$assets $(brew_linux_asset "$t")"
  done
  # shellcheck disable=SC2086 # asset names are space-free and split on purpose
  brew_fetch "$from" "$tmp" $assets >"$tmp/fetched.sums" 2>"$tmp/missing" || rc=$?
  case "$rc" in
    0) ;;
    3)
      if [ "$dev" -eq 1 ]; then
        sed 's/^missing: /not in this dev tap: /' "$tmp/missing" >&2
      else
        cat "$tmp/missing" >&2
        printf '%s: a release formula needs all five assets, and %s of them are not at %s, so no formula is rendered\n' \
          "$DEVBIN_NAME" "$(grep -c . "$tmp/missing")" "$from" >&2
        rm -rf "$tmp"
        return 3
      fi
      ;;
    *)
      cat "$tmp/missing" >&2
      rm -rf "$tmp"
      exit "$rc"
      ;;
  esac
  staged="$STAGE_DIR/SHA256SUMS.txt"
  if [ "$base" = release ] && [ -f "$staged" ]; then
    for a in $(brew_macos_assets); do
      got="$(brew_sum "$tmp/fetched.sums" "$a")"
      want="$(brew_sum "$staged" "$a")"
      if [ "$got" != "$want" ]; then
        rm -rf "$tmp"
        die "$a at $from hashes to ${got:-nothing}, and $staged records ${want:-nothing}: the release does not serve the bytes checksum proved, so no formula is rendered"
      fi
    done
  fi
  commit="$(brew_source_commit "$version" "$dev")" || {
    rm -rf "$tmp"
    exit 1
  }
  brew_render_formula "$version" "$revision" "$base" "$tmp/fetched.sums" "$commit" || {
    rm -rf "$tmp"
    exit 1
  }
  rm -rf "$tmp"
}

# THE CASK -- Casks/intent-app.rb, so Intent.app installs and upgrades through
# Homebrew as the CLI does (issue 0634, hv's ruling). `brew upgrade` used to move
# the CLI and leave the app on whatever version was unzipped by hand.
#
# RENDERED AND PUSHED BESIDE THE FORMULA, by the one push below, so a release
# never carries a formula at one version and a cask at another. Its sha256 is
# read from the bytes the release serves at cut time, as every formula hash is,
# and with the release's own base it must equal the one SHA256SUMS.txt records.
CASK_TOKEN="intent-app"
# The floor is the app's deployment target, `deploymentTarget: macOS` in
# native/macos/Intent/project.yml, in the symbol brew names that release by. A
# bare symbol is the floor: `brew style` refuses the quoted ">= :tahoe" form.
APP_MACOS_FLOOR=":tahoe"

# brew_render_cask <version> <sha256> -- PURE. The cask for <version>, its zip at
# the release's own download URL.
brew_render_cask() {
  cat <<CASK
cask "$CASK_TOKEN" do
  version "$1"
  sha256 "$2"

  url "https://github.com/$REPO/releases/download/v#{version}/$APP_ASSET"
  name "Intent"
  desc "Menubar app for Intent's steel thread process"
  homepage "https://github.com/$REPO"

  depends_on formula: "$BREW_TAP/intent"
  depends_on macos: $APP_MACOS_FLOOR

  app "Intent.app"
end
CASK
}

# brew_cask <version> <from> -- IMPURE. Casks/intent-app.rb on stdout, its sha256
# read from the bytes of APP_ASSET at <from>, which is the release's download URL
# for <version> when <from> is empty. When the zip is not there the return is 3,
# with it named and its URL on stderr, and nothing is rendered. The cask's url is
# always the release's: a dev tap's file:// base proves the hash, never the URL.
brew_cask() {
  local version="$1" from="$2" base tmp rc=0 got want staged
  require_tool curl
  if [ -z "$from" ]; then
    base=release
    from="https://github.com/$REPO/releases/download/v$version"
  else
    base="$from"
  fi
  tmp="$(mktemp -d)" || die "could not make a directory to fetch $APP_ASSET into"
  brew_fetch "$from" "$tmp" "$APP_ASSET" >"$tmp/fetched.sums" 2>"$tmp/missing" || rc=$?
  case "$rc" in
    0) ;;
    3)
      cat "$tmp/missing" >&2
      printf '%s: the cask needs %s, and it is not at %s, so no cask is rendered\n' "$DEVBIN_NAME" "$APP_ASSET" "$from" >&2
      rm -rf "$tmp"
      return 3
      ;;
    *)
      cat "$tmp/missing" >&2
      rm -rf "$tmp"
      exit "$rc"
      ;;
  esac
  got="$(brew_sum "$tmp/fetched.sums" "$APP_ASSET")"
  staged="$STAGE_DIR/SHA256SUMS.txt"
  if [ "$base" = release ] && [ -f "$staged" ]; then
    want="$(brew_sum "$staged" "$APP_ASSET")"
    if [ "$got" != "$want" ]; then
      rm -rf "$tmp"
      die "$APP_ASSET at $from hashes to ${got:-nothing}, and $staged records ${want:-nothing}: the release does not serve the bytes checksum proved, so no cask is rendered"
    fi
  fi
  rm -rf "$tmp"
  brew_render_cask "$version" "$got"
}

# brew_push_tap <formula> <cask> <version> <how> -- IMPURE. Commit <formula> to
# the tap as Formula/intent.rb and <cask> as Casks/<CASK_TOKEN>.rb, in one commit,
# and push it, with <how> saying in the commit what made it. THE ONE PUSH TO THE
# TAP, and `int brew publish` its one caller, which asks at hv's terminal first;
# `int macos publish` stopped pushing with ST0081 WP-02. One commit, so the tap
# never holds a formula and a cask from different releases (issue 0634).
brew_push_tap() {
  local formula="$1" cask="$2" version="$3" how="$4" work
  require_tool gh "Install it with: brew install gh"
  gh repo view "$TAP" >/dev/null 2>&1 || die "cannot reach the tap $TAP -- check 'gh auth status'"
  work="$(mktemp -d)" || die "could not make a directory to clone the tap into"
  gh repo clone "$TAP" "$work/tap" -- --quiet >/dev/null 2>&1 || {
    rm -rf "$work"
    die "could not clone $TAP"
  }
  mkdir -p "$work/tap/Formula" "$work/tap/Casks" || {
    rm -rf "$work"
    die "could not make Formula/ and Casks/ in the clone of $TAP"
  }
  printf '%s\n' "$formula" >"$work/tap/Formula/intent.rb" || {
    rm -rf "$work"
    die "could not write the formula into the clone of $TAP"
  }
  printf '%s\n' "$cask" >"$work/tap/Casks/$CASK_TOKEN.rb" || {
    rm -rf "$work"
    die "could not write the cask into the clone of $TAP"
  }
  git -C "$work/tap" add Formula/intent.rb "Casks/$CASK_TOKEN.rb" || {
    rm -rf "$work"
    die "could not stage the formula and the cask in the clone of $TAP"
  }
  git -C "$work/tap" commit -q -m "intent $version

$how

(C) hello@matthewsinclair.com" || {
    rm -rf "$work"
    die "nothing to commit -- the formula and the cask are unchanged"
  }
  git -C "$work/tap" push -q origin HEAD || {
    rm -rf "$work"
    die "could not push the formula and the cask to $TAP"
  }
  rm -rf "$work"
}
